+# UI feedback and motion
+
+The chat view uses the shared `SiriAIControlFeedback` and
+`SiriAIInteractionFeedback` for buttons and editable fields, including progress
+details, message disclosures, conversation search and titles, modal scrims and
+dynamically added wake words. Existing scene-owned click and semantic audio clips
+use the selected audio endpoint and its bounded voice pool. Sound volume, mute,
+haptics, disabled controls and microphone sound protection retain their existing
+behavior.
+
+The latest-message button starts an analytic, critically damped spring in pixel
+space after layout. Streaming updates and window resizing move the destination
+without restarting the spring. Repeated clicks preserve momentum. A press, drag
+or wheel navigation takes control immediately; streaming then preserves the
+reader's position until they return to the bottom or activate the button again.
+Changing conversations cancels the old scroll. Reduced motion and lower quality
+tiers finish the motion immediately. Editor previews retain immediate navigation.
+
+History paging and the viewport-sized message pool remain bounded. Returning
+from an older page loads the latest page at the previous relative scroll offset
+and animates within that page; it does not instantiate or traverse every hidden
+message. The latest affordance fades in and out, rejects clicks while closing,
+and composes its visibility with press feedback instead of competing for scale.
+
+Progress/error sheets preserve their presented geometry and velocity when
+reopened during dismissal. The old dismissal callback is discarded. Existing
+settings, conversation windows, menus, toggles and drawer transitions continue
+to use the shared interruptible motion system.
+
+Regression coverage is in `SiriAIExperienceFeedbackTests` and
+`SiriAIExperienceAudioTests`, alongside the existing motion, history, input and
+audio routing suites. Allocation checks explicitly skip Unity runtimes with an
+unavailable allocation counter. The integration timing check measures CPU motion
+work, not total GPU frame time or physical speaker output.
